# Key material and credentials. Nothing here belongs in git: on Cloud Run every secret arrives
# from Secret Manager at deploy time (`--set-secrets`, deploy/cloudrun/README.md), so a key file
# in this tree is always either a mistake or a local experiment. These patterns exist so one
# dropped in the working directory by accident cannot be committed — before them, this file had
# no secret patterns at all.
#
# *service-account*.json earns its place even though that README argues you should never need
# one: GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS is a PATH, so the escape hatch for pinning a specific
# identity is a mounted key file — and that is precisely the file someone copies into a checkout
# "just to test push locally".
#
# !deploy/**/*.env.example matches nothing today; it is here so that an example file added
# later stays committable rather than being silently swallowed by *.env.
*.pem
*.p8
*.p12
*.jks
*.key
*-private-key*
*service-account*.json
.env
*.env
!deploy/**/*.env.example

# Claude Code local state. Neither of these is project content.
#
# settings.local.json is per-machine permission grants that every session appends to. It was
# tracked until 2026-08-20, which meant each session's grants landed in the branch history as
# diff noise and every worktree raced the same file. `git rm --cached` untracked it; the file
# stays on disk and keeps working. Anything meant to be SHARED goes in .claude/settings.json,
# which is still tracked deliberately.
#
# worktrees/ holds agent worktrees checked out inside the repo. Each carries a .git FILE, not a
# .git directory, so git sees ordinary files rather than a nested repo it would skip — while
# untracked and unignored, a stray `git add -A` would have committed ~1.4 GB of duplicated
# source and build output.
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/worktrees/
